<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">British Military Administration of Libya</h1><p class="st-meta">1943 CE – 1951 CE · capital Tripoli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British wartime and post-war rule over Tripolitania and Cyrenaica, which ended with Libya's UN-sponsored independence in 1951.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">British Commonwealth</a> forces drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> in November 1942 and took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-tripoli">Tripoli</a> in January 1943. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> set up military administrations in both provinces, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> held the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/fezzan-ghadames-military-territory">Fezzan-Ghadames Military Territory</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under the laws of war, the British largely retained Italian law and administration and many Italian officials, while allowing Libyan political parties to form. The future of the former colony was debated by the victorious powers; in 1949 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bevin-sforza-plan">Bevin-Sforza plan</a> for trusteeships was rejected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">UN General Assembly</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In November 1949 the UN decided that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> should become independent by 1952, sending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adrian-pelt">Adrian Pelt</a> as commissioner. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-cyrenaica">Emirate of Cyrenaica</a> in 1949, and Libya gained independence as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-libya">Kingdom of Libya</a> on 24 December 1951.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-military-administration-of-libya.jpg" alt="British Military Administration of Libya" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1943 CE – 1951 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tripoli</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Italian, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Tripolitania and Cyrenaica</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1947&amp;focus=british-military-administration-of-libya" aria-label="British Military Administration of Libya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5018 983 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Military Administration of Libya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5338 1239L5338 1239M5338 1239L4997 651M5338 1239L5338 1239"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5338 1239h0M4997 651h0M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5338 1239h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5338 1239h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tripoli. <a href="/atlas?year=1947&amp;focus=british-military-administration-of-libya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
